{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 places these catalytic domains in the same Actin-like ATPase domain superfamily, c.55.1 (SUNID 53067), but in distinct families: Actin/HSP70, c.55.1.1; Hexokinase, c.55.1.3; and Glycerol kinase, c.55.1.4. This superfamily belongs to the Ribonuclease H-like motif fold, c.55, within the alpha/beta class, c.

Their shared superfamily supports remote common ancestry of the domains despite sequence and functional divergence. It supports an evolutionarily conserved nucleotide-binding catalytic framework adapted to different uses: ATP hydrolysis associated with actin dynamics and HSP70 chaperone cycling, versus ATP-dependent phosphorylation of sugars by hexokinase and glycerol by glycerol kinase. Shared fold alone would establish structural similarity without necessarily establishing homology; shared class is still broader. Family membership indicates closer evolutionary relationships, but even the Actin/HSP70 family does not imply identical cellular functions.

The relationship does not justify transferring substrate specificity, an exact catalytic mechanism, cellular role, or whole-protein architecture between these proteins. A distant member can inform modeling of the conserved core, but reliable active-site or loop modeling requires adequate alignment and structural coverage. Functional transfer needs additional sequence and active-site evidence, ligand-bound structures, and biochemical substrate/activity measurements.","evidence_used":["Pretrained model knowledge","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/children?limit=100"]}]}
